.TH WLCB 1 "2026-11-08" "notcoreutils" "notcoreutils Manual"
.SH NAME
wlcb \- Wayland clipboard manager with history
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wlcb
[\fB\-p\fR] \fBcopy\fR [\fITEXT\fR...]
.br
.B wlcb paste
.br
.B wlcb list
.br
.B wlcb select
\fIN\fR
.br
.B wlcb
[\fB\-p\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B wlcb
is the Wayland twin of
.BR xcb (1):
the same commands and the same behaviour, so scripts can swap one for the
other. It uses the data\-control protocol (\fIext\-data\-control\-v1\fR if the
compositor offers it, otherwise \fIzwlr_data_control_manager_v1\fR), which
needs no focused window, so it works under dwl and other wlroots compositors.
It watches the clipboard for text (\fItext/plain;charset=utf\-8\fR and
\fItext/plain\fR), keeps a RAM ring buffer of history, and re\-activates an old
entry by owning the selection itself. When the application that owned the
clipboard exits, the last entry is re\-owned so it is not lost.
.PP
The first
.BR copy / paste /TUI
invocation auto\-starts a background daemon with a Unix socket at
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/wlcb.sock
(or
.I /tmp/.wlcb-<uid>.sock
if that is unset). History lives only in the daemon's RAM.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B copy \fR[\fITEXT\fR...]
Copy TEXT (or stdin, if no TEXT is given) onto the clipboard.
.TP
.B paste
Print the current clipboard contents.
.TP
.B list
Print the full history, most recent first.
.TP
.B select \fIN\fR
Re\-activate history entry N.
.TP
.B (no args)
Interactive TUI: fuzzy\-search history, Enter to re\-activate an entry.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-p
When this invocation starts the daemon, also record the PRIMARY
(middle\-click) selection into history. Ignored if a daemon is already running.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.B WAYLAND_DISPLAY
selects the compositor, as usual.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR xcb (1)
